@meta
id: 15-12
title: Gather the matches, in order
chapter: 15
type: lesson
skills: global-move
par: 25
minutes: 5
vscode: cut and paste seven lines one at a time, keeping count
open: registry.ts

@brief
Someone added the last few services to the DI registry by putting each
`import` directly above its `container.register`, so the file is now
imports and registrations interleaved. The convention is all imports at
the top, all registrations below, and the registrations in the order
they were written, because the registry order matters for this
framework.

Move every `container.register` line to the bottom of the file, in the
order they currently appear. The imports stay where they are.

You reversed a file last lesson by moving every line to the top. The
same idea, pointed at the other end of the file, does something
different: moving each matching line to the bottom, one after another,
collects them there and keeps their order.

@keys
| `:g/pattern/m$` | move every matching line to the end of the file, in order |
| `:m$` | move the current line to the end |

@hints
The pattern needs to catch the seven registration lines and nothing
else. They all start the same way.
--
Each matched line goes to the end, after the one before it. The result
is the matches, in the order they were found, at the bottom.
--
`:g/^container/m$` and Enter.

@solution
    :g/^container/m$<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Twenty-one keystrokes, and the registrations arrive at the bottom in
the order they left.

`m$` and `m0` are the two ends of the same trick, and they behave
differently in a way that matters. `m$` appends: the first match goes
to the bottom, the second match goes below it, and the order is kept.
`m0` prepends: the first match goes to the top, the second goes above
it, and the order is reversed. That is exactly why `:g/^/m0` reverses
a file. So: gather at the bottom when order matters. If you must gather
at the top, run `:g/pattern/m0` and then run it again, the second pass
un-reverses the first. It looks like a trick and it is one, but it is
the honest consequence of the mechanism.

The registry order rule is real, by the way. Producers, then
repositories, then providers, then consumers, so that nothing is
resolved before the thing it depends on. Editing tools that preserve
order are not a nicety in a file like this.

@start registry.ts
import { container } from 'tsyringe';
import { OrderRepository } from '../domain/order/order.repository';
import { OrderProvider } from '../domain/order/order.provider';
import { OrderResolver } from '../domain/order/order.resolver';
import { CustomerRepository } from '../domain/customer/customer.repository';
container.register('OrderRepository', { useClass: OrderRepository });
container.register('CustomerRepository', { useClass: CustomerRepository });
import { CustomerProvider } from '../domain/customer/customer.provider';
container.register('OrderProvider', { useClass: OrderProvider });
container.register('CustomerProvider', { useClass: CustomerProvider });
import { PaymentService } from '../infrastructure/services/payment.service';
container.register('PaymentService', { useClass: PaymentService });
import { EmailService } from '../infrastructure/services/email.service';
container.register('EmailService', { useClass: EmailService });
import { OrderConsumer } from '../domain/order/order.consumer';
container.register('OrderConsumer', { useClass: OrderConsumer });

@expected registry.ts
import { container } from 'tsyringe';
import { OrderRepository } from '../domain/order/order.repository';
import { OrderProvider } from '../domain/order/order.provider';
import { OrderResolver } from '../domain/order/order.resolver';
import { CustomerRepository } from '../domain/customer/customer.repository';
import { CustomerProvider } from '../domain/customer/customer.provider';
import { PaymentService } from '../infrastructure/services/payment.service';
import { EmailService } from '../infrastructure/services/email.service';
import { OrderConsumer } from '../domain/order/order.consumer';
container.register('OrderRepository', { useClass: OrderRepository });
container.register('CustomerRepository', { useClass: CustomerRepository });
container.register('OrderProvider', { useClass: OrderProvider });
container.register('CustomerProvider', { useClass: CustomerProvider });
container.register('PaymentService', { useClass: PaymentService });
container.register('EmailService', { useClass: EmailService });
container.register('OrderConsumer', { useClass: OrderConsumer });
